1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,000
It's a pet and a poulte.

2
00:00:03,001 --> 00:00:06,000
Plucked, processed, and pardoned.

3
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:07,000
What are these again?

4
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:08,000
Turkey testicles.

5
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:09,000
That's what I thought.

6
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:13,000
Now, it's the turkey on Modern Marbles.

7
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:26,000
The turkey.

8
00:00:26,001 --> 00:00:28,000
An odd-looking bird from North America,

9
00:00:28,001 --> 00:00:32,000
evolved from pheasant ancestors.

10
00:00:32,001 --> 00:00:36,000
European explorers to America came upon the bird in the 1500s

11
00:00:36,001 --> 00:00:40,000
and mistakenly identified it as a type of guinea fowl called a turkey cock,

12
00:00:40,001 --> 00:00:44,000
which got its name after it was imported to Europe via turkey.

13
00:00:44,001 --> 00:00:48,000
The cock was eventually dropped, and the turkey had a name that stuck.

14
00:00:48,001 --> 00:00:52,000
Whether or not it graced the pilgrim's first Thanksgiving is in dispute,

15
00:00:52,001 --> 00:00:56,000
but it's certain that Benjamin Franklin lobbied for it to become America's

16
00:00:56,001 --> 00:00:58,700
Franklin lobbied for it to become America's national bird.

17
00:01:03,440 --> 00:01:07,180
Still, turkeys have never been accused of being the brightest animals in the barn.

18
00:01:08,660 --> 00:01:14,680
After all, when frightened, domesticated turkeys will often bunch up in piles and suffocate one another.

19
00:01:16,040 --> 00:01:19,820
But there's one old story about turkey intelligence that's probably a myth.

20
00:01:20,200 --> 00:01:25,220
I've always heard the story told where a turkey, he'll just stick his head up when it rains

21
00:01:25,221 --> 00:01:26,580
and drowns.

22
00:01:27,560 --> 00:01:30,740
I've never seen that, 23 years of growing turkeys.

23
00:01:31,020 --> 00:01:33,780
If it starts raining, they come back in the house

24
00:01:33,781 --> 00:01:34,960
just like me and you would.

25
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:37,280
The primary breed raised domestically

26
00:01:37,281 --> 00:01:38,820
is called the Nicholas White.

27
00:01:39,020 --> 00:01:39,720
All right.

28
00:01:39,780 --> 00:01:41,560
This is a hen, this is a tom.

29
00:01:41,760 --> 00:01:43,220
They're both the exact same age.

30
00:01:43,920 --> 00:01:47,040
Notable differences seen in these two sexes

31
00:01:47,041 --> 00:01:50,140
are the small, smooth profile of the hen head

32
00:01:50,141 --> 00:01:51,740
and a much smaller snood.

33
00:01:52,440 --> 00:01:53,980
That's right, a snood.

34
00:01:53,981 --> 00:01:56,540
And that fleshy thing hanging below the neck?

35
00:01:56,541 --> 00:01:57,820
That's a wattle.

36
00:01:57,821 --> 00:01:59,980
To turkeys, they're sexy.

37
00:01:59,981 --> 00:02:03,660
The wattle at the base of the neck, the snood,

38
00:02:03,661 --> 00:02:07,100
the different head coloration, all used to attract females

39
00:02:07,101 --> 00:02:08,220
for breeding in the wild.

40
00:02:08,221 --> 00:02:11,740
The eyes are offset on the side of the head to avoid predation.

41
00:02:11,741 --> 00:02:16,140
A turkey can typically see almost a 360-degree radius around them.

42
00:02:16,141 --> 00:02:19,100
A beak is basically used to procure food in the wild

43
00:02:19,101 --> 00:02:22,380
and defend itself against other turkeys.

44
00:02:22,381 --> 00:02:29,380
Sometimes hens have been known to actually sacrifice themselves in the wild to protect their brood, their poops and offspring.

45
00:02:29,381 --> 00:02:35,380
Domesticated turkeys are raised for one primary purpose, to be eaten by people.

46
00:02:35,381 --> 00:02:40,380
Every year, Americans consume more than 250 million of them.

47
00:02:40,381 --> 00:02:44,380
The biggest consumer worldwide? Would you believe Israel?

48
00:02:44,381 --> 00:02:49,380
The process of getting them to the table starts at breeding farms,

49
00:02:49,381 --> 00:02:53,844
breeding farms, like the Sessar Farm in Goldsboro, North Carolina.

50
00:02:53,845 --> 00:02:55,940
Here, readers have devised ways

51
00:02:55,941 --> 00:03:00,639
to manipulate mother nature so their hens can lay enough eggs to meet demand.

52
00:03:00,640 --> 00:03:01,920
And there's not a tom

53
00:03:01,921 --> 00:03:04,754
in sight. These birds don't mate.

54
00:03:04,755 --> 00:03:09,820
That's because since the 1960s, domesticated hens have been

55
00:03:09,821 --> 00:03:11,506
artificially inseminated.

56
00:03:11,507 --> 00:03:16,160
It's enabled us to produce much more turkey with much fewer resources

57
00:03:16,161 --> 00:03:17,720
than by natural mating.

58
00:03:18,520 --> 00:03:23,140
We can have one tom for every 20 to 25 hens now.

59
00:03:23,280 --> 00:03:26,200
Natural mating would be about one tom

60
00:03:26,201 --> 00:03:27,940
for every four to five hens.

61
00:03:28,940 --> 00:03:31,660
In this case, the domesticated toms get busy

62
00:03:31,661 --> 00:03:33,360
at North Carolina State University,

63
00:03:33,760 --> 00:03:35,560
where their semen is manually extracted.

64
00:03:37,760 --> 00:03:40,000
The semen is then sent to the hen farm,

65
00:03:40,600 --> 00:03:42,060
put inside a plastic straw,

66
00:03:42,480 --> 00:03:43,920
and inserted into the hen.

67
00:03:43,921 --> 00:03:50,920
The process is repeated for each bird in the flock approximately once a week.

68
00:03:50,921 --> 00:03:54,920
In the wild, turkeys mate in the spring.

69
00:03:54,921 --> 00:03:58,920
The increase in daylight hours triggers the hens' natural laying mechanism.

70
00:03:58,921 --> 00:04:02,920
By adjusting the light in the barn, breeders are able to mimic nature

71
00:04:02,921 --> 00:04:06,920
and artificially trigger domestic hens into laying eggs year-round.

72
00:04:06,921 --> 00:04:10,920
The reason we're coming into egg production now is we've restricted them.

73
00:04:10,921 --> 00:04:16,600
from any lot other than six hours a day since they were 16 weeks old.

74
00:04:17,600 --> 00:04:21,600
We get them here, we boost the lives to 14 and a half, 15 hours.

75
00:04:22,080 --> 00:04:23,840
Within two weeks they'll start producing eggs.

76
00:04:24,800 --> 00:04:27,940
To increase the turkey's egg production to about four a week

77
00:04:27,941 --> 00:04:30,640
instead of the almost 12 laid annually in the wild,

78
00:04:31,240 --> 00:04:33,500
breeders alter the hen's natural nesting instinct.

79
00:04:34,900 --> 00:04:39,100
She lays a clutch of eggs in the wild and she sets on them to try to incubate them.

80
00:04:39,101 --> 00:04:43,460
Well, that's why we wanted to break, because she starts doing that, she's going to quit laying.

81
00:04:44,380 --> 00:04:46,660
So we keep these birds moved away from the nest.

82
00:04:47,300 --> 00:04:52,120
Basically, the guys push the birds out to collect the eggs, allows another turkey to come in.

83
00:04:55,140 --> 00:05:01,140
After the eggs are collected, they're given a quick wash and sent to the turkey hatchery a few miles down the road.

84
00:05:01,141 --> 00:05:10,280
We bring the eggs in from the breeder farm and keep them at 62 degrees with a fairly

85
00:05:10,281 --> 00:05:11,472
high humidity.

86
00:05:11,473 --> 00:05:17,520
The egg will remain dormant until we actually put it in the incubation.

87
00:05:17,521 --> 00:05:20,620
It takes four weeks to hatch.

88
00:05:20,621 --> 00:05:27,260
For 25 days, the eggs sit inside incubators at 98 degrees Fahrenheit while they develop.

89
00:05:27,261 --> 00:05:31,260
and place them atop a bed of lights for a peek inside.

90
00:05:31,360 --> 00:05:35,260
This allows spotters to pull any eggs that failed to incubate.

91
00:05:35,360 --> 00:05:39,000
After about a month, we've got a look at a baby turkey,

92
00:05:39,100 --> 00:05:41,200
or poult, as it enters the world.

93
00:05:41,300 --> 00:05:46,220
The temperature and humidity are all automatically controlled.

94
00:05:46,320 --> 00:05:50,120
And what we're looking at now is going to be partially hatched

95
00:05:50,220 --> 00:05:52,120
poult.

96
00:05:52,220 --> 00:05:54,120
You can see them through here.

97
00:05:54,121 --> 00:05:56,120
You can see them through here.

98
00:05:56,121 --> 00:05:58,120
They're not all hatched out yet.

99
00:05:58,121 --> 00:06:00,120
Where's my cart?

100
00:06:09,120 --> 00:06:14,120
The polt has got a very hard, what they call a pipping tooth.

101
00:06:14,121 --> 00:06:18,120
A little slight yellow hard calcium right on the end of the beak.

102
00:06:18,121 --> 00:06:20,120
It's called a pipping tooth.

103
00:06:20,121 --> 00:06:22,120
And this is basically what they pip.

104
00:06:22,121 --> 00:06:23,560
They tip all the way around.

105
00:06:23,561 --> 00:06:28,820
They spin inside that shell, and then the shell, pop it out.

106
00:06:28,821 --> 00:06:32,600
Here's one that has pipped all the way around the shell,

107
00:06:32,601 --> 00:06:35,260
and it's just getting ready to pop the cap up.

108
00:06:39,140 --> 00:06:41,340
There we are.

109
00:06:41,341 --> 00:06:44,200
It'll take you about an hour to look just like these,

110
00:06:44,201 --> 00:06:47,080
all nice, dry, and fluffy.

111
00:06:47,081 --> 00:06:50,620
Thus begins the life of a domesticated turkey.

112
00:06:50,621 --> 00:06:56,260
The goal from here is to keep the poults healthy and make them plump.

113
00:06:56,261 --> 00:07:00,060
The first stop for these poults is the Tracy Price Brooder Farm.

114
00:07:00,061 --> 00:07:04,700
The first week is the main week, from my opinion, on a turkey.

115
00:07:04,701 --> 00:07:07,240
It's just like a newborn baby.

116
00:07:07,241 --> 00:07:09,600
This little poults right here, when he's delivered to me,

117
00:07:09,601 --> 00:07:12,980
he will weigh approximately an ounce and a half.

118
00:07:12,981 --> 00:07:16,620
And in 35 to 38 days, when he leaves here, if all goes well,

119
00:07:16,621 --> 00:07:18,220
he'll weigh at least four pounds.

120
00:07:20,620 --> 00:07:26,860
Fattening up the poults means starting them off on a diet consisting of 28% protein.

121
00:07:26,861 --> 00:07:32,400
It's made primarily with corn, soybean meal, vitamins and minerals.

122
00:07:32,401 --> 00:07:35,420
And they will be fed this for approximately two weeks.

123
00:07:35,421 --> 00:07:39,700
At that time the protein levels will change according to what the birds need with the

124
00:07:39,701 --> 00:07:42,440
age and the weight that they're growing.

125
00:07:42,441 --> 00:07:47,140
Like all birds, the turkey has an unusual way of processing food.

126
00:07:47,141 --> 00:07:52,680
It passes through the digestive tract to the crop, where muscle tissue softens it up.

127
00:07:52,681 --> 00:07:57,540
It then passes to the gizzard, where tiny stones that the bird has instinctively swallowed

128
00:07:57,541 --> 00:07:59,380
grind it into a pulp.

129
00:07:59,381 --> 00:08:05,260
The pulp then passes into the small intestine, where nutrients enter the bloodstream.

130
00:08:05,261 --> 00:08:09,580
Pults need to maintain a body temperature near 105 degrees Fahrenheit.

131
00:08:09,581 --> 00:08:14,320
So once again, technology fills in for nature.

132
00:08:14,321 --> 00:08:18,660
The main thing in the middle here is a brooder, it's a heater.

133
00:08:18,661 --> 00:08:22,680
That serves as the turkey's mother.

134
00:08:22,681 --> 00:08:25,080
That's why you see a lot of them congregated in the middle.

135
00:08:25,081 --> 00:08:26,760
They're there to feel the heat.

136
00:08:26,761 --> 00:08:29,940
That makes them think that that's their mother up there above them.

137
00:08:29,941 --> 00:08:34,960
For the next five weeks, the poults will be carefully nurtured until they're strong enough

138
00:08:34,961 --> 00:08:40,280
for transfer to a finishing farm.

139
00:08:40,281 --> 00:08:43,400
Hens go to one farm, toms to another.

140
00:08:43,401 --> 00:08:47,100
at the Hardy Cross Creek Farm in LaGrange, North Carolina.

141
00:08:47,101 --> 00:08:50,100
When we walk in, they're all gonna follow us.

142
00:08:50,101 --> 00:08:53,240
If you notice right now, they're gonna start coming to us.

143
00:08:53,241 --> 00:08:55,380
They're kind of checking us out,

144
00:08:55,381 --> 00:08:58,980
and which, that's a real healthy sign there.

145
00:08:58,981 --> 00:09:01,620
I love to hear this gobble.

146
00:09:01,621 --> 00:09:04,880
The happier they are, the happier I am.

147
00:09:04,881 --> 00:09:08,300
This is all about making money.

148
00:09:08,301 --> 00:09:10,760
A healthy bottom line depends on keeping the turkeys

149
00:09:10,761 --> 00:09:13,160
comfortable and well-fed.

150
00:09:13,161 --> 00:09:15,960
Pans and sprinklers keep the birds cool.

151
00:09:15,961 --> 00:09:19,700
Gravity-fed dispensers keep the feed bins full.

152
00:09:19,701 --> 00:09:25,040
We have hens in domestic production that go over 25 pounds, and our toms up to 46, 47

153
00:09:25,041 --> 00:09:26,400
pounds sometimes.

154
00:09:26,401 --> 00:09:30,540
A wild turkey to reach 25 pounds may take four years.

155
00:09:30,541 --> 00:09:34,480
Now, we can accomplish that in about 12 to 15 weeks.

156
00:09:34,481 --> 00:09:39,240
Consumer demand for big breasts and meaty thighs has cost the domesticated turkey one

157
00:09:39,241 --> 00:09:40,240
of its powers.

158
00:09:40,241 --> 00:09:44,020
With the evolution of the high meat yields on these turkeys, we've lost the ability to

159
00:09:44,021 --> 00:09:48,700
fly because the weight is much greater than the wings and the holliness of the body cavity

160
00:09:48,701 --> 00:09:51,000
that would be required for flight.

161
00:09:51,001 --> 00:09:56,680
Despite the extra pounds and a life without any hens, the toms still have their male pride

162
00:09:56,681 --> 00:09:57,680
and urges.

163
00:09:57,681 --> 00:10:03,500
If you look over, over to my left here, you'll see this turkey over here strutting.

164
00:10:03,501 --> 00:10:04,500
Look at his snoot.

165
00:10:04,501 --> 00:10:06,000
He's hanging down.

166
00:10:06,001 --> 00:10:07,880
He's strutting.

167
00:10:07,881 --> 00:10:11,160
He's trying to intimidate another turkey over here.

168
00:10:11,161 --> 00:10:13,240
This is how he's going to attract the hen.

169
00:10:13,241 --> 00:10:15,880
This is how he puts fear in the other turkeys.

170
00:10:15,881 --> 00:10:21,660
18 to 20 weeks after birth, the turkeys are trucked to a processing facility.

171
00:10:21,661 --> 00:10:30,120
In this case, the Butterball processing plant in Mount Olive, North Carolina.

172
00:10:30,121 --> 00:10:34,360
Soon after their arrival, the turkeys are humanely slaughtered and de-feathered, and

173
00:10:34,361 --> 00:10:37,600
then cleaned with an antimicrobial solution.

174
00:10:37,601 --> 00:10:41,860
Then the turkeys are put on a shackle line and hoisted along to be systematically processed

175
00:10:41,861 --> 00:10:43,740
for market.

176
00:10:43,741 --> 00:10:48,720
Birds processed at this facility are all toms and are destined to become a variety of products,

177
00:10:48,721 --> 00:10:50,720
like deli meats, bacon and sausage.

178
00:10:50,721 --> 00:10:55,840
In turkey processing it's important that we use the whole bird.

179
00:10:55,841 --> 00:10:59,960
We use this turkey neck not only for a product that we put in the whole bird that you can

180
00:10:59,961 --> 00:11:04,200
make gravy with at Thanksgiving, but we also use it mostly as a raw material for ground

181
00:11:04,201 --> 00:11:05,200
turkey.

182
00:11:05,201 --> 00:11:08,260
Edible organ meat is also chilled down to 40 degrees.

183
00:11:08,800 --> 00:11:15,800
And then collected here, the heart, the liver, and the gizzard,

184
00:11:16,340 --> 00:11:19,780
one of each are then placed into a giblet pack.

185
00:11:20,300 --> 00:11:23,440
You may recognize this from your last year's Thanksgiving turkey.

186
00:11:23,900 --> 00:11:25,340
Hopefully you didn't cook it in the bird.

187
00:11:26,400 --> 00:11:27,480
Actually, this is for gravy.

188
00:11:27,820 --> 00:11:29,280
Ask grandma what to do with this packet.

189
00:11:29,380 --> 00:11:30,160
She'll know what to do.

190
00:11:30,640 --> 00:11:34,080
After the birds have been gutted, they're rehung for easy butchering.

191
00:11:35,200 --> 00:11:37,460
These are 40 pound birds, and these guys

192
00:11:37,461 --> 00:11:38,600
were hanging these all day long,

193
00:11:38,601 --> 00:11:40,640
so they're really building the muscles.

194
00:11:40,641 --> 00:11:44,980
The first cut in the butchering process is the breast cut.

195
00:11:44,981 --> 00:11:46,980
We separate the front and the back halves.

196
00:11:48,880 --> 00:11:51,120
Birds then swing around to the wing line.

197
00:11:52,320 --> 00:11:53,720
This is where the wing is removed

198
00:11:53,721 --> 00:11:55,420
from the front half of the bird.

199
00:11:55,421 --> 00:11:57,180
It comes off in two sections.

200
00:11:57,181 --> 00:11:59,560
You have a two joint wing, which is cut off

201
00:11:59,561 --> 00:12:01,160
from the far end of this line,

202
00:12:01,161 --> 00:12:03,500
and then we have a drumette that's cut off.

203
00:12:03,501 --> 00:12:05,500
The drumette is a really intricate cut.

204
00:12:05,501 --> 00:12:07,500
It takes a lot of practice to get right,

205
00:12:07,501 --> 00:12:09,500
and it takes a good sharp knife.

206
00:12:09,501 --> 00:12:13,500
The remaining turkey meat is then cut in half.

207
00:12:13,501 --> 00:12:16,500
The top portion moves to the cut-up line.

208
00:12:16,501 --> 00:12:19,500
The leg and thigh processor handles the bottom half.

209
00:12:19,501 --> 00:12:22,500
The leg processor makes four cuts,

210
00:12:22,501 --> 00:12:26,500
and five pieces of meat are retrieved from those four cuts.

211
00:12:26,501 --> 00:12:30,500
As the bird enters the machine, the back is broke.

212
00:12:30,501 --> 00:12:32,740
Two cuts are made along each side of the back,

213
00:12:33,100 --> 00:12:36,000
and then the drums are removed from the thighs,

214
00:12:36,260 --> 00:12:38,740
yielding two drums, two thighs, and one back.

215
00:12:39,800 --> 00:12:43,000
And so goes the process of repairing America's turkey,

216
00:12:43,860 --> 00:12:46,400
17 pounds per person every year.

217
00:12:47,440 --> 00:12:49,440
The process peaks at Thanksgiving.

218
00:12:51,160 --> 00:12:54,980
That's also when the phones at the Butterball Turkey Talk Line light up.

219
00:12:54,981 --> 00:12:56,320
Butterball Turkey Talk Line, can I help you?

220
00:12:56,680 --> 00:12:59,060
And a group of women known as the Turkey Experts

221
00:12:59,061 --> 00:13:01,620
help cooks prepare their Thanksgiving Day bird.

222
00:13:01,621 --> 00:13:03,600
When I spray the turkey spears.

223
00:13:03,601 --> 00:13:06,400
The majority of calls can be answered with a tip

224
00:13:06,401 --> 00:13:09,900
from the Butterball cooking mantra, called the three T's.

225
00:13:09,901 --> 00:13:15,200
The first one is thaw, which means thawed correctly,

226
00:13:15,201 --> 00:13:18,220
do it in a cold way, cold refrigerator, cold water.

227
00:13:18,221 --> 00:13:20,820
The second T is to use a thermometer,

228
00:13:20,821 --> 00:13:23,720
and you want to cook the turkey until it's 170 in the breast,

229
00:13:23,721 --> 00:13:25,020
180 in the thigh.

230
00:13:25,021 --> 00:13:28,160
And our third T is what we call our two-hour rule.

231
00:13:28,161 --> 00:13:30,360
two hours after that turkey comes out of the oven,

232
00:13:30,460 --> 00:13:33,100
we want you to have it cut up back in your refrigerator

233
00:13:33,101 --> 00:13:35,020
getting cold for those great leftovers.

234
00:13:36,160 --> 00:13:37,760
About that second tea,

235
00:13:38,340 --> 00:13:40,360
it's the key to keeping turkey meat moist.

236
00:13:41,640 --> 00:13:42,820
But how does it work?

237
00:13:45,040 --> 00:13:47,840
Once the temperature reaches 180 degrees Fahrenheit,

238
00:13:49,360 --> 00:13:51,600
the muscle fibers begin to break up,

239
00:13:52,420 --> 00:13:54,300
causing proteins to unravel

240
00:13:54,301 --> 00:13:56,400
and the muscle meat to become tender.

241
00:13:56,401 --> 00:14:01,780
If the meat continues to cook, the muscle proteins coagulate and the meat becomes dry.

242
00:14:02,820 --> 00:14:04,600
But white meat cooks faster than dark.

243
00:14:05,700 --> 00:14:12,940
By covering the breast with foil or something similar, the heat is shielded, allowing both white and dark meat to cook at the same rate.

244
00:14:14,500 --> 00:14:18,480
Before the turkey ladies can talk turkey, they're sent to Butterball University.

245
00:14:19,280 --> 00:14:23,060
And no one graduates without first learning how to carve the perfect slice of breast meat.

246
00:14:23,061 --> 00:14:28,960
First, disjoint the leg and thigh and pull them apart.

247
00:14:30,160 --> 00:14:33,280
Then, make a cut right above the wing just below the breast.

248
00:14:35,460 --> 00:14:36,840
Now begin slicing.

249
00:14:37,240 --> 00:14:41,940
And as you can see, those slices will fall right off.

250
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:45,620
Not all turkey dishes are so traditional.

251
00:14:46,080 --> 00:14:51,320
One restaurant in Indiana occasionally goes off the menu for some more creative dishes.

252
00:14:51,321 --> 00:14:53,160
These are turkey testicles.

253
00:14:54,120 --> 00:14:58,880
We like to just serve these and we just call them turkey fries and leave it at that.

254
00:15:00,120 --> 00:15:03,820
Domesticated male turkeys weigh 50% more than their wild counterparts.

255
00:15:04,580 --> 00:15:07,000
Brain weight is about a third less than in wild turkeys.

256
00:15:07,680 --> 00:15:09,820
Most affected is the area that controls vision.

257
00:15:10,920 --> 00:15:13,400
The turkey will return on Modern Marvels.

258
00:15:15,340 --> 00:15:17,660
Welcome to America's turkey restaurant.

259
00:15:18,480 --> 00:15:21,540
The Strongbow Inn in Valparaiso, Indiana.

260
00:15:22,860 --> 00:15:25,440
The Strongbow, which opened in the 1940s,

261
00:15:25,740 --> 00:15:28,060
was built across the highway from its own turkey farm.

262
00:15:29,520 --> 00:15:32,960
The farm is long gone, but the menu is still the same.

263
00:15:33,600 --> 00:15:35,260
All turkey, all the time.

264
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:38,300
From its giant turkey wings and pot pies

265
00:15:38,301 --> 00:15:41,320
to its famous turkey schnitzel and gobbler's delight,

266
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:44,920
the Strongbow has inspired people to come from all over America

267
00:15:44,921 --> 00:15:48,900
to sink their teeth into some of the most succulent turkey dishes in the land.

268
00:15:49,140 --> 00:15:50,260
It's all about the turkey.

269
00:15:51,260 --> 00:15:55,560
We're kind of a destination restaurant, but with everybody eating healthier,

270
00:15:56,000 --> 00:16:01,980
they tend to go for more real food or choices that are healthier for them.

271
00:16:02,080 --> 00:16:03,920
So turkey, of course, would fit that bill.

272
00:16:04,640 --> 00:16:07,460
The Strongbow is owned and operated by Chef Russ Adams.

273
00:16:08,460 --> 00:16:10,240
Here's where he cracks open a mystery.

274
00:16:10,940 --> 00:16:13,600
What's the difference between chicken and turkey eggs?

275
00:16:13,601 --> 00:16:16,600
With the turkey, it all starts with the egg.

276
00:16:16,601 --> 00:16:18,600
And so I've got a turkey egg here.

277
00:16:18,601 --> 00:16:20,600
You can see it's speckled.

278
00:16:20,601 --> 00:16:23,600
It's a little bit larger than a chicken egg.

279
00:16:23,601 --> 00:16:25,600
And here I've got a chicken egg.

280
00:16:25,601 --> 00:16:26,600
You can see the difference between the two.

281
00:16:26,601 --> 00:16:27,600
This one's pure white.

282
00:16:27,601 --> 00:16:29,600
This one's got the speckling on it.

283
00:16:29,601 --> 00:16:31,600
These are primarily all double-yolked eggs.

284
00:16:31,601 --> 00:16:33,600
The reason we don't use so many turkey eggs in cooking

285
00:16:33,601 --> 00:16:35,600
is, quite frankly, they're tough to get.

286
00:16:35,601 --> 00:16:37,600
You know, the turkeys just don't produce enough eggs

287
00:16:37,601 --> 00:16:38,600
to make it worth their while.

288
00:16:38,601 --> 00:16:41,600
They're better off to use them to produce turkeys.

289
00:16:41,601 --> 00:16:42,600
Of course, there's our double yolk.

290
00:16:42,601 --> 00:16:47,480
They would also be expensive, upwards of seven times the price of chicken eggs.

291
00:16:47,481 --> 00:16:51,240
That's why the egg is about the only part of the turkey that's not on the menu here.

292
00:16:51,241 --> 00:16:56,160
But since he's got the eggs and the frying pan is nice and hot, let's ask the chef to try one.

293
00:16:56,161 --> 00:17:01,160
So why don't we take it on over to the table here and we'll try it and see if we've got a difference.

294
00:17:01,161 --> 00:17:04,120
That's the big key, whether or not the flavor is the same.

295
00:17:04,121 --> 00:17:07,560
So far it looks about just like a chicken egg, doesn't it?

296
00:17:12,600 --> 00:17:15,600
Tastes like an egg to me.

297
00:17:15,601 --> 00:17:18,600
It's time to spice things up a bit.

298
00:17:18,601 --> 00:17:20,600
These should help.

299
00:17:20,601 --> 00:17:23,600
Turkey testicles, anyone?

300
00:17:23,601 --> 00:17:26,600
These are quite large, actually, for normal turkey testicles.

301
00:17:26,601 --> 00:17:29,600
I don't know what size birds these came off of.

302
00:17:29,601 --> 00:17:31,600
Right now, they're rather jelly-like,

303
00:17:31,601 --> 00:17:36,600
but after they're cooked, they have a different texture,

304
00:17:36,601 --> 00:17:38,600
but it's not what you would think.

305
00:17:38,601 --> 00:17:39,600
Okay.

306
00:17:39,601 --> 00:17:42,600
Okay, and moving on, the testicles are sliced,

307
00:17:43,180 --> 00:17:44,260
soaked in a milk bath,

308
00:17:45,080 --> 00:17:46,560
dipped in seasoned breadcrumbs,

309
00:17:47,140 --> 00:17:48,600
and sauteed in a pan.

310
00:17:49,640 --> 00:17:51,240
Use quite a bit of butter for this.

311
00:17:52,160 --> 00:17:54,220
I think the people who would eat turkey fries

312
00:17:54,221 --> 00:17:55,720
are probably bar goers.

313
00:17:55,800 --> 00:17:56,800
Usually after a few beers,

314
00:17:56,880 --> 00:17:59,480
I think it probably would be more apt to try them.

315
00:18:00,420 --> 00:18:01,920
I'm going to let these brown on one side,

316
00:18:01,980 --> 00:18:02,860
and then we're going to turn it over

317
00:18:02,861 --> 00:18:04,380
and let them brown on the other.

318
00:18:04,820 --> 00:18:06,640
And then serve them to the brave guy

319
00:18:06,641 --> 00:18:07,760
with a duck shirt on.

320
00:18:09,600 --> 00:18:16,600
It's good.

321
00:18:16,601 --> 00:18:17,600
What are these again?

322
00:18:17,601 --> 00:18:19,600
These are turkey testicles.

323
00:18:19,601 --> 00:18:20,600
That's what I thought.

324
00:18:20,601 --> 00:18:21,600
Are they really?

325
00:18:21,601 --> 00:18:22,600
What do they taste like?

326
00:18:22,601 --> 00:18:23,600
What do you think?

327
00:18:23,601 --> 00:18:26,600
A little bit like popcorn shrimp.

328
00:18:26,601 --> 00:18:31,600
While our good-natured diner finishes off his plate of testicles, let's move on to turkey

329
00:18:31,601 --> 00:18:32,600
bacon.

330
00:18:32,601 --> 00:18:37,600
Have you ever wondered how manufacturers are able to give it the look of pork bacon?

331
00:18:37,601 --> 00:18:40,600
Butterball let us in on the secret.

332
00:18:40,601 --> 00:18:44,600
This is the beginning of the turkey bacon process.

333
00:18:44,601 --> 00:18:49,600
What we're looking here, they are loading the grinder.

334
00:18:49,601 --> 00:18:53,600
The frozen wing gets dumped into this grinder.

335
00:18:53,601 --> 00:18:59,600
Then it comes out, it gets grinded to a half an inch,

336
00:18:59,601 --> 00:19:02,600
is the size of particle we are doing here.

337
00:19:02,601 --> 00:19:05,600
Once the white wing meat is ground to the optimal size,

338
00:19:05,601 --> 00:19:08,600
it needs to be cooled to the proper temperature.

339
00:19:08,601 --> 00:19:14,600
We are adding at this point ice into our ground white meat.

340
00:19:14,601 --> 00:19:23,600
It is very important in the emulsion process to keep the meat always cold, as cold as possible.

341
00:19:23,601 --> 00:19:30,600
That's the only way to make a good emulsion, to make sure that the fat particles are suspended into the protein.

342
00:19:30,601 --> 00:19:34,600
We're going to start blending these ice into the meat.

343
00:19:34,601 --> 00:19:37,600
Then we're going to add some water.

344
00:19:37,601 --> 00:19:41,600
Salt, sugar and smoke flavoring are added for taste.

345
00:19:41,601 --> 00:19:44,600
20 minutes later, the mixture enters the emulsifier,

346
00:19:44,601 --> 00:19:47,600
where all the ingredients are bound together.

347
00:19:47,601 --> 00:19:51,600
The white meat is getting transferred to the emulsifier.

348
00:19:51,601 --> 00:19:56,600
By this way, the emulsifier gets fed into it.

349
00:19:56,601 --> 00:20:04,100
Once the meat is in the emulsifier, what we're doing is reducing the meat particles to about

350
00:20:04,101 --> 00:20:06,600
1.2 millimeters in size.

351
00:20:06,601 --> 00:20:13,760
Now it's just a smooth paste that is going to replicate what will be the fat portion

352
00:20:13,761 --> 00:20:16,280
of regular pork bacon.

353
00:20:16,281 --> 00:20:21,080
Meanwhile, dark meat from turkey drumsticks has been mixed separately in another area

354
00:20:21,081 --> 00:20:22,080
of the plant.

355
00:20:22,081 --> 00:20:26,480
What we have here is larger pieces of meat, your white face.

356
00:20:26,580 --> 00:20:31,780
You want it to be very fine, but the dark meat is the one that is going to be giving

357
00:20:31,781 --> 00:20:34,280
you the texture and the bite of the bacon.

358
00:20:35,380 --> 00:20:37,780
Put the dark and white meat together and...

359
00:20:38,480 --> 00:20:42,880
The secret for making the turkey bacon look like pork bacon.

360
00:20:43,700 --> 00:20:47,600
What we do here is we combine the white face of the turkey.

361
00:20:47,601 --> 00:20:52,560
This is an emulsion with a dark mix of the turkey.

362
00:20:52,720 --> 00:20:58,460
It will both come together and make that characteristic marbling that you see in the turkey bacon.

363
00:21:01,200 --> 00:21:09,620
Just cook, slice, package, and off it goes to the grocery store bacon shelf.

364
00:21:09,621 --> 00:21:18,620
Compared to an average slice of pork bacon, turkey bacon has 65% less fat and 10 fewer

365
00:21:18,621 --> 00:21:19,620
calories.

366
00:21:19,621 --> 00:21:22,620
How about turkey burgers?

367
00:21:22,621 --> 00:21:26,620
Much of the ground turkey comes from the meat on the turkey drumstick.

368
00:21:26,621 --> 00:21:29,620
This is our drum machine.

369
00:21:29,621 --> 00:21:35,620
This machine takes a drum hole, squeezes the meat together, has a piston that pushes the

370
00:21:35,621 --> 00:21:38,620
bone out the bottom of the drum machine.

371
00:21:38,621 --> 00:21:46,720
The bone exits the bottom of the off-off, and the meat comes out the back and makes

372
00:21:46,721 --> 00:21:49,940
a premium ground turkey product.

373
00:21:49,941 --> 00:21:54,140
The drum meat is ground up with up to 40% of other cuts, depending on the fat percentage

374
00:21:54,141 --> 00:21:55,700
of the final product to be.

375
00:21:55,701 --> 00:22:01,800
Even though the majority of our product comes to us pretty much bone-free, at this stage

376
00:22:01,801 --> 00:22:06,260
of the process we go through a secondary bone removal, so any tendon or any other tough

377
00:22:06,261 --> 00:22:11,080
tissue that might not be appetizing comes through a bone removal process.

378
00:22:11,580 --> 00:22:12,560
That's what you see here.

379
00:22:13,580 --> 00:22:17,920
Ground turkey just might end up in turkey burgers like those served back at the Strongbow Inn.

380
00:22:18,580 --> 00:22:21,760
We top it with sauteed onions and shiitake mushrooms.

381
00:22:25,400 --> 00:22:27,220
And there you have our double turkey burger.

382
00:22:28,120 --> 00:22:33,360
From poulte to plate in less than five months, courtesy of the American turkey industry.

383
00:22:33,361 --> 00:22:39,200
While the domesticated turkey has been raised to fit man's needs, the wild turkey hasn't

384
00:22:39,201 --> 00:22:41,120
changed for millions of years.

385
00:22:41,121 --> 00:22:48,180
But if you want to eat one, you'll have to call it first.

386
00:22:48,181 --> 00:22:54,220
On Christmas Eve 1968, astronauts aboard Apollo 8 dined on turkey with gravy and cranberry

387
00:22:54,221 --> 00:22:55,500
sauce.

388
00:22:55,501 --> 00:23:01,300
The turkey was not dehydrated, but preserved in a special moisture pouch instead.

389
00:23:01,301 --> 00:23:05,700
The turkey will return on Modern Marvels.

390
00:23:05,701 --> 00:23:08,240
It's mating season in the Montana woods,

391
00:23:08,241 --> 00:23:11,680
and the wild toms, or gobblers, are getting frisky.

392
00:23:11,681 --> 00:23:13,540
The gobblers want to get with the hens and vice versa.

393
00:23:13,541 --> 00:23:14,860
So the gobbler will just basically

394
00:23:14,861 --> 00:23:20,560
follow those hens around and try to ward off any other gobblers.

395
00:23:20,561 --> 00:23:23,240
The gobblers struck their stuff against the competition

396
00:23:23,241 --> 00:23:25,860
in an elegant mating ritual that places a premium

397
00:23:25,861 --> 00:23:28,200
on beauty and attitude.

398
00:23:28,201 --> 00:23:29,940
Probably one of the most beautiful sights

399
00:23:29,941 --> 00:23:32,080
you can see is when you see one of those gobblers out there,

400
00:23:32,081 --> 00:23:35,400
his head tucked back, red, white, and blue.

401
00:23:35,401 --> 00:23:38,640
And he's got that tail fan stuck out there,

402
00:23:38,641 --> 00:23:40,280
just corralling hens.

403
00:23:40,281 --> 00:23:42,480
Very pretty sight.

404
00:23:42,481 --> 00:23:44,700
These noble birds have inhabited America

405
00:23:44,701 --> 00:23:47,760
long before the domestication of animals.

406
00:23:47,761 --> 00:23:51,060
But today, mating season is also hunting season.

407
00:23:53,720 --> 00:23:56,600
While the gobblers are focused on attracting hens,

408
00:23:56,601 --> 00:23:58,620
hunters quietly move toward the mating area.

409
00:23:59,940 --> 00:24:02,300
One sudden move could mean the end of the hunt.

410
00:24:03,440 --> 00:24:08,800
Wild turkey can fly upwards, I believe, 35 miles an hour or more,

411
00:24:09,200 --> 00:24:13,780
and they take flight very quick, and they can also run very fast, amazingly fast.

412
00:24:13,880 --> 00:24:16,500
So they're built for speed, they're built for getting away.

413
00:24:17,960 --> 00:24:23,200
What we're trying to do is convince that gobbler that he needs to come over here and leave those hens behind.

414
00:24:23,740 --> 00:24:25,060
So that's the game right there.

415
00:24:25,660 --> 00:24:28,340
The best way to attract a gobbler is to sound like a hen.

416
00:24:28,341 --> 00:24:33,140
So hunters use an arsenal of man-made calls to lure the turkeys to them.

417
00:24:33,141 --> 00:24:42,740
What we have here is a piece of latex stretched between a metal horseshoe to hold it taut.

418
00:24:43,780 --> 00:24:47,068
And it actually fits up against the roof of your mouth.

419
00:24:47,069 --> 00:24:49,460
And by using tongue pressure and letting

420
00:24:49,461 --> 00:24:53,540
loose and huffing air through it, you can get a somewhat, some people are better than others,

421
00:24:53,541 --> 00:24:56,280
but you can get that hen call, the yelp of the hen.

422
00:24:56,281 --> 00:24:57,640
That goes something like that.

423
00:25:13,520 --> 00:25:14,660
No response.

424
00:25:15,400 --> 00:25:18,260
The first known turkey call came from Native Americans,

425
00:25:19,120 --> 00:25:21,040
a yelper made from a hollow wing bone.

426
00:25:23,540 --> 00:25:33,320
A tube caller mimics the mating sound of a wild gobbler.

427
00:25:33,321 --> 00:25:41,940
It's designed to entice a gobbler to come in to protect its turf.

428
00:25:41,941 --> 00:25:45,480
The most widely used call is the shrieking sound from a box call.

429
00:25:45,481 --> 00:25:51,940
It's a friction type call, rubbing the lid across the edge of the wood.

430
00:25:51,941 --> 00:25:58,940
a pitch and it's a louder call, reaches out further.

431
00:26:03,880 --> 00:26:07,980
That was just kind of like an excited hymn, like, doggone it, where are you big boy?

432
00:26:07,981 --> 00:26:14,980
You know, I'm looking for you.

433
00:26:14,981 --> 00:26:19,640
He's making his move in a different direction than I'm going.

434
00:26:19,641 --> 00:26:23,940
So he won this match, I think.

435
00:26:26,580 --> 00:26:30,120
In the early 1900s, the match was all but over.

436
00:26:30,920 --> 00:26:37,220
Centuries of habitat destruction and subsistence hunting had taken a devastating toll on the wild turkey population.

437
00:26:38,300 --> 00:26:40,300
Commercial harvesting nearly finished them off.

438
00:26:41,040 --> 00:26:41,780
There were no laws.

439
00:26:41,781 --> 00:26:45,540
I mean, hens were taken and there wasn't any seasons,

440
00:26:45,780 --> 00:26:48,940
so they were taken all times of the year.

441
00:26:49,500 --> 00:26:53,440
By the Great Depression, just 30,000 wild turkeys remained

442
00:26:53,441 --> 00:26:55,160
scattered throughout the United States.

443
00:26:55,920 --> 00:26:59,640
The 1937 Wildlife Restoration Act changed that.

444
00:27:00,520 --> 00:27:02,480
It imposed taxes on hunting equipment

445
00:27:02,481 --> 00:27:06,900
and used them to establish conservation and habitat restoration programs.

446
00:27:07,780 --> 00:27:09,280
Starting in the 1950s,

447
00:27:09,281 --> 00:27:15,180
Hunters in the National Wild Turkey Federation did their share too, raising millions of additional dollars.

448
00:27:16,260 --> 00:27:18,340
Turkey populations rebounded dramatically.

449
00:27:19,420 --> 00:27:25,400
Now more than 7 million turkeys from five different subspecies roam the wilds of North America.

450
00:27:26,440 --> 00:27:28,440
And the hunt is back on.

451
00:27:29,260 --> 00:27:32,400
We're revisiting right now the golden age of wild turkey hunting.

452
00:27:32,640 --> 00:27:36,680
They're back. Most states have a one-bird limit or two-bird limit.

453
00:27:36,681 --> 00:27:44,040
And the hunters are the ones that brought the laws, demanded the laws to be here.

454
00:27:44,041 --> 00:27:47,780
Of course, hunters aren't the only predators in the forest.

455
00:27:47,781 --> 00:27:52,340
To stay safe at night, wild turkeys use the natural habitat to keep themselves out of

456
00:27:52,341 --> 00:27:53,340
harm's way.

457
00:27:53,341 --> 00:27:54,860
They're a roosting bird.

458
00:27:54,861 --> 00:27:59,120
And so they actually have a, many of them have a favorite roost tree.

459
00:27:59,121 --> 00:28:01,860
They feel certainly a lot safer up in the trees.

460
00:28:01,861 --> 00:28:03,940
There's predators on the ground at night.

461
00:28:03,941 --> 00:28:07,100
It's just an instinctive thing they do.

462
00:28:07,101 --> 00:28:09,700
During the day, wild turkeys venture from their nests

463
00:28:09,701 --> 00:28:11,600
to gather food.

464
00:28:11,601 --> 00:28:13,380
And because they're omnivores, they'll

465
00:28:13,381 --> 00:28:16,160
eat just about anything.

466
00:28:16,161 --> 00:28:19,080
They'll eat everything from a plant.

467
00:28:19,081 --> 00:28:20,940
I mean, right here at our feet, you'll see many things.

468
00:28:20,941 --> 00:28:25,180
They'll eat the little blades of grass and the seeds on top.

469
00:28:25,181 --> 00:28:28,360
They like berries, any kind of leaf matter.

470
00:28:28,361 --> 00:28:31,540
On average, wild hens grow to about eight pounds,

471
00:28:31,541 --> 00:28:33,540
gobblers to around 18 pounds.

472
00:28:42,540 --> 00:28:46,540
One characteristic unique to the wild turkey comes in handy during mating season.

473
00:28:46,541 --> 00:28:49,483
Mature gobblers will have spurs half-inch or better.

474
00:28:49,484 --> 00:28:52,540
These here measure in about three-quarters of an inch.

475
00:28:52,541 --> 00:28:59,540
It's a defensive tool because they do fight for the rights for the hens.

476
00:28:59,541 --> 00:29:02,373
hens. They also have here a beard.

477
00:29:02,374 --> 00:29:06,540
Actually it's a type of feather, although it looks

478
00:29:07,360 --> 00:29:10,616
like a hair that grows out of his chest.

479
00:29:10,617 --> 00:29:14,360
The beard has no significance that we know of.

480
00:29:14,361 --> 00:29:19,300
Many people look at these as a trophy part of a wild turkey.

481
00:29:19,301 --> 00:29:25,540
Native Americans prize wild turkey feathers for their beauty and spiritual significance.

482
00:29:25,541 --> 00:29:29,900
We do pluck many of these birds and we send them off to Native Americans for ceremonial

483
00:29:29,901 --> 00:29:31,960
programs and dances and that type of thing.

484
00:29:31,961 --> 00:29:34,260
They're sought after by the Native Americans.

485
00:29:34,261 --> 00:29:37,780
We try to comply with the wishes there and use as much as we can.

486
00:29:37,781 --> 00:29:42,900
The meat of course is delicious and we use that ourselves.

487
00:29:42,901 --> 00:29:46,420
And in case you're wondering how it tastes compared to a domestic.

488
00:29:46,421 --> 00:29:48,220
I personally like it better.

489
00:29:48,221 --> 00:29:51,460
It's not as dry.

490
00:29:51,461 --> 00:29:54,640
It's excellent table fare.

491
00:29:54,641 --> 00:29:59,640
This here is also excellent eating.

492
00:30:01,640 --> 00:30:04,640
Inside the gizzard are the remnants from the turkey's last meal.

493
00:30:05,640 --> 00:30:07,640
Remember those rocks we told you about earlier?

494
00:30:07,641 --> 00:30:09,640
Well, here's the proof.

495
00:30:09,641 --> 00:30:15,640
These are actually aids for them to help digest their food.

496
00:30:15,641 --> 00:30:18,640
The rocks are eaten. It's a natural thing.

497
00:30:18,641 --> 00:30:21,640
They're a scratching, foraging bird.

498
00:30:21,641 --> 00:30:23,880
And of course, they're gonna pick up rocks naturally,

499
00:30:23,881 --> 00:30:25,220
no doubt.

500
00:30:25,221 --> 00:30:26,120
If you're gonna eat the gizzard,

501
00:30:26,121 --> 00:30:27,580
which there's no reason why you wouldn't want

502
00:30:27,581 --> 00:30:29,640
to eat the gizzard, you actually will skin

503
00:30:29,641 --> 00:30:34,580
the inner wall of the stomach away from the meat.

504
00:30:37,960 --> 00:30:40,400
And it's not a hard process at all.

505
00:30:42,200 --> 00:30:44,160
And you have a pretty nice, clean gizzard

506
00:30:44,161 --> 00:30:45,860
after some washing.

507
00:30:45,861 --> 00:30:47,800
National Wild Turkey Federation, our volunteers,

508
00:30:47,801 --> 00:30:50,240
spent a lot of time and effort to help bring back

509
00:30:50,241 --> 00:30:55,240
this beautiful bird, but we also enjoy the hunt and the harvest.

510
00:30:55,241 --> 00:31:00,240
And we eat the bird that we so much like to hunt.

511
00:31:00,241 --> 00:31:02,240
Nothing really is wasted.

512
00:31:02,241 --> 00:31:05,240
With hunters leading the way in conservation,

513
00:31:05,241 --> 00:31:09,240
the future of these beautiful wild birds is secure.

514
00:31:09,241 --> 00:31:12,240
But their numbers will always pale in comparison to the millions

515
00:31:12,241 --> 00:31:15,240
of domesticated turkeys raised each year,

516
00:31:15,241 --> 00:31:19,240
numbers so large that even their by-products are in industry.

517
00:31:19,241 --> 00:31:20,980
It's like a snowstorm in here.

518
00:31:23,220 --> 00:31:26,680
While wild turkey hens lay an average of 9 to 12 eggs a year,

519
00:31:27,120 --> 00:31:29,340
only 50% of all their eggs hatch.

520
00:31:30,040 --> 00:31:32,320
Between 12 and 24 hours after hatching,

521
00:31:32,760 --> 00:31:35,180
poults begin to follow the hens away from their nests.

522
00:31:36,000 --> 00:31:38,240
The turkey will return on Modern Marvels.

523
00:31:42,300 --> 00:31:43,940
Nearly every day of the week,

524
00:31:44,720 --> 00:31:47,220
Changing World Technologies in Carthage, Missouri,

525
00:31:47,221 --> 00:31:52,220
receives 250 tons of turkey byproducts inside this hopper.

526
00:31:54,560 --> 00:31:57,600
Changing world technologies is part of a growing industry

527
00:31:57,601 --> 00:31:59,680
geared to transforming these byproducts

528
00:31:59,681 --> 00:32:01,400
into useful goods.

529
00:32:01,401 --> 00:32:03,820
In this case, fuel oil, the same stuff

530
00:32:03,821 --> 00:32:06,780
that's pulled from the earth, and fertilizer.

531
00:32:06,781 --> 00:32:08,920
As you can see in here, we have a mixture

532
00:32:08,921 --> 00:32:12,380
of all kinds of feedstock that we get in.

533
00:32:12,381 --> 00:32:17,060
This leading edge here is mostly feathers.

534
00:32:17,061 --> 00:32:22,800
The things that look like balloons are a combination of intestines and lungs.

535
00:32:23,760 --> 00:32:26,960
Of course, you have the feet, the beaks.

536
00:32:27,840 --> 00:32:31,400
The byproducts follow a conveyor to a set of rotating knives

537
00:32:31,401 --> 00:32:33,260
that chop the parts into a slurry,

538
00:32:33,920 --> 00:32:37,040
which is then pumped into a 300-degree Fahrenheit tank.

539
00:32:37,860 --> 00:32:41,060
Here, the solids, like bones and beaks, are separated from the liquids.

540
00:32:41,760 --> 00:32:46,820
The bone is heavier than the liquid, so it falls to the bottom of the tank.

541
00:32:47,060 --> 00:32:54,060
we pull that bone material off in a real rough cut.

542
00:32:57,060 --> 00:33:02,060
It's nothing more than the bones with the marrow pulled out.

543
00:33:02,061 --> 00:33:06,060
You can see it's kind of a brownish gray,

544
00:33:06,061 --> 00:33:09,060
looks kind of like a silly putty.

545
00:33:09,061 --> 00:33:11,060
Loaded with phosphate,

546
00:33:11,061 --> 00:33:16,060
the pasty material is sold as a fertilizer.

547
00:33:16,061 --> 00:33:21,040
Changing World Technologies also processes crude oil inside a pressurized set of pipes,

548
00:33:21,700 --> 00:33:25,480
much like what the Earth did to the dinosaurs, except thousands of times faster.

549
00:33:26,320 --> 00:33:28,280
It's called thermal conversion processing.

550
00:33:30,340 --> 00:33:33,700
Pipes pump the slurry to a 500-degree Fahrenheit reactor.

551
00:33:34,520 --> 00:33:36,860
The pressure in the reactor is set to 700 pounds.

552
00:33:38,760 --> 00:33:44,460
For the next 30 minutes, the complex long-chain hydrogen and carbon molecules laced with minerals

553
00:33:44,461 --> 00:33:47,300
break down into shorter and lighter molecules

554
00:33:47,301 --> 00:33:50,180
that are identical to those found in diesel fuel oil.

555
00:33:53,520 --> 00:33:55,000
Once the pressure is lowered,

556
00:33:55,540 --> 00:33:57,660
pipes direct the slurry into a centrifuge

557
00:33:57,661 --> 00:34:00,380
where the oil is separated from any remaining water.

558
00:34:02,000 --> 00:34:09,380
What you see here is the finished oil product

559
00:34:09,381 --> 00:34:12,580
as it's being separated away from the water.

560
00:34:14,460 --> 00:34:21,900
The Changing World Technologies plant is now at near capacity, pumping out over 7 million gallons of diesel fuel a year.

561
00:34:23,980 --> 00:34:26,520
And the potential for more is staggering.

562
00:34:27,820 --> 00:34:34,880
If factories processed poultry byproducts, including the 1.5 million tons of turkey byproducts now discarded each year,

563
00:34:35,240 --> 00:34:38,260
they could produce 30 million gallons of fuel annually.

564
00:34:38,620 --> 00:34:42,080
That's enough to fill the United States' fuel needs for a day and a half.

565
00:34:42,081 --> 00:34:44,560
It started as turkey.

566
00:34:46,420 --> 00:34:52,840
Meanwhile, turkey feathers are about to fly inside Philadelphia University's textile laboratory.

567
00:34:53,780 --> 00:34:57,020
These are turkey feathers.

568
00:34:57,920 --> 00:34:59,640
You can see they're relatively fine.

569
00:35:00,180 --> 00:35:05,560
What they do is they run it through basically a shredding unit, a grinding unit, which will remove the quill.

570
00:35:05,900 --> 00:35:11,720
The fibers themselves have then been shredded a little bit, so we end up with a very fine, soft material.

571
00:35:11,721 --> 00:35:18,520
it's very fluffy in fact we could probably use this as as a absorbent material in diaper applications

572
00:35:20,120 --> 00:35:26,600
but these feathers are being tested for another purpose home insulation here we have our fibers

573
00:35:26,601 --> 00:35:32,200
coming through it's being fed underneath this roll underneath this roll into this roll which

574
00:35:32,201 --> 00:35:39,320
is covered up we've got a 50 50 blend of turkey feather fibers and polyolefin fibers

575
00:35:41,720 --> 00:35:44,360
Looks very good, it's blended through the thickness.

576
00:35:44,361 --> 00:35:47,160
We've got feathers going through the thickness.

577
00:35:47,161 --> 00:35:48,620
They're on the other side also.

578
00:35:49,620 --> 00:35:52,440
It's a little dusty, it's a little messy.

579
00:35:54,200 --> 00:35:57,200
That's what happens, yeah, it's like a snowstorm in here.

580
00:36:00,000 --> 00:36:01,400
If I had a little bit of tar on me,

581
00:36:01,401 --> 00:36:02,700
I'd be feathered already.

582
00:36:05,480 --> 00:36:07,040
Here we have the end of our roll.

583
00:36:07,041 --> 00:36:08,680
What we'll do is we'll cut the paper.

584
00:36:09,680 --> 00:36:10,720
Oh, excuse me.

585
00:36:10,721 --> 00:36:13,480
and we'll take it over to bonding.

586
00:36:13,481 --> 00:36:16,400
At this point, if you look at this part right here,

587
00:36:16,401 --> 00:36:18,560
you can see that it pulls apart very easily.

588
00:36:18,561 --> 00:36:20,260
He looks like he's talking about tar and feathered.

589
00:36:20,261 --> 00:36:21,100
Wow.

590
00:36:23,240 --> 00:36:24,960
There's really nothing holding these fibers together

591
00:36:24,961 --> 00:36:26,940
except a little bit of fiber-to-fiber friction.

592
00:36:26,941 --> 00:36:28,080
That's not going to be strong enough

593
00:36:28,081 --> 00:36:30,180
to hold everything together.

594
00:36:30,181 --> 00:36:32,800
Sending the roll through a 160-degree oven

595
00:36:32,801 --> 00:36:34,620
heats up the synthetic fibers,

596
00:36:34,621 --> 00:36:37,840
bonding them to the turkey feathers.

597
00:36:37,841 --> 00:36:38,680
So here it is.

598
00:36:38,681 --> 00:36:40,080
You can see as I'm pulling it off the screen

599
00:36:40,081 --> 00:36:42,820
that it has some stability to it now.

600
00:36:42,821 --> 00:36:44,620
It actually has some strength.

601
00:36:44,621 --> 00:36:48,320
We can handle it without it falling apart.

602
00:36:48,321 --> 00:36:50,260
All we would have to do is add a flame retardant,

603
00:36:50,261 --> 00:36:53,020
and then we would have a fabric made with turkey feathers

604
00:36:53,021 --> 00:36:54,600
that would be a great insulating product,

605
00:36:54,601 --> 00:36:57,320
ready for use by the consumer.

606
00:36:57,321 --> 00:36:59,260
The university's successful tests

607
00:36:59,261 --> 00:37:03,000
may make insulation manufacturers take notice.

608
00:37:03,001 --> 00:37:05,840
If they bite, the four billion turkey feathers

609
00:37:05,841 --> 00:37:08,880
dumped in landfills every year may find their way

610
00:37:08,881 --> 00:37:11,680
inside the walls of American homes.

611
00:37:11,681 --> 00:37:15,380
It's all thanks to the domesticated turkey.

612
00:37:15,381 --> 00:37:18,220
One California man gives thanks in a different way,

613
00:37:18,221 --> 00:37:20,120
by giving his turkeys the opportunity

614
00:37:20,121 --> 00:37:22,960
to live a long and happy life as pets.

615
00:37:22,961 --> 00:37:25,400
Isn't that right?

616
00:37:25,401 --> 00:37:27,640
Turkey dung is being used as a fuel source

617
00:37:27,641 --> 00:37:29,340
in electric power plants.

618
00:37:29,341 --> 00:37:33,200
One such plant in Minnesota provides 55 megawatts of power

619
00:37:33,201 --> 00:37:37,040
using fuel derived primarily from almost 500,000 tons

620
00:37:37,041 --> 00:37:38,740
of turkey dung per year.

621
00:37:38,741 --> 00:37:40,620
It powers about 40,000 homes.

622
00:37:41,140 --> 00:37:43,400
The turkey will return on Modern Marvels.

623
00:37:45,940 --> 00:37:48,100
Can a turkey be a good pet?

624
00:37:48,760 --> 00:37:50,620
This California vegetarian thinks so.

625
00:37:51,480 --> 00:37:54,080
He has 40 turkeys and a couple of dozen chickens

626
00:37:54,081 --> 00:37:55,760
running wild on his Malibu property.

627
00:37:56,780 --> 00:38:00,000
People come on the property and they just start laughing.

628
00:38:00,520 --> 00:38:01,260
It's fun.

629
00:38:01,620 --> 00:38:03,220
I've always been fascinated with animals.

630
00:38:03,980 --> 00:38:05,320
They just bring me a lot of happiness.

631
00:38:05,321 --> 00:38:09,320
Some of the pet turkeys were rescued from shelters.

632
00:38:09,321 --> 00:38:14,320
The rest were born here and will live their entire lives foraging in the grass and trying

633
00:38:14,321 --> 00:38:15,320
to stay out of the pool.

634
00:38:15,321 --> 00:38:18,320
But there's one turkey that has the run of the place.

635
00:38:18,321 --> 00:38:20,320
This is my pet turkey, Thanks.

636
00:38:20,321 --> 00:38:25,320
And I rescued her from a place that was going to sell her for me.

637
00:38:25,321 --> 00:38:29,320
And she had a sister named Giving, but Giving unfortunately died.

638
00:38:29,321 --> 00:38:32,320
So, Thanks is still with me and great turkey.

639
00:38:32,321 --> 00:38:37,160
She's getting a little sleepy now because she could fall asleep in my lap if I pet her.

640
00:38:37,880 --> 00:38:43,600
Fortunately for thanks, she usually stays awake long enough to walk around the house until she finds the dog food dish.

641
00:38:46,060 --> 00:38:47,900
Who doesn't have a turkey as a pet, huh?

642
00:38:49,980 --> 00:38:51,760
Come on outside. Let's go.

643
00:38:52,560 --> 00:38:54,460
Oh, here we go. You've had enough.

644
00:38:54,461 --> 00:39:05,220
Aside from the occasional coyote, the turkeys are free from predators and worry, giving

645
00:39:05,221 --> 00:39:07,640
their owner the chance to observe their behavior.

646
00:39:07,641 --> 00:39:17,420
They all have really distinct personalities and they're very loud.

647
00:39:17,421 --> 00:39:22,800
I think that a lot of people see turkeys as a commodity or as meat, but I see them as

648
00:39:22,801 --> 00:39:30,040
these amazing creatures who have personalities, who have a spirit and love life.

649
00:39:30,041 --> 00:39:34,360
This spring, turkey love brought another generation of turkeys to the property.

650
00:39:34,361 --> 00:39:40,431
I had a pleasant surprise. One of my turkeys hid in the yard and had 16 babies.

651
00:39:40,432 --> 00:39:41,200
So we have

652
00:39:41,201 --> 00:39:49,360
an addition to the family, which is, it's a lot. It's a lot.

653
00:39:49,361 --> 00:39:54,700
For most turkeys, the only path to this kind of freedom is through a presidential pardon.

654
00:39:54,701 --> 00:40:00,040
Every year, the White House stages the pre-Thanksgiving Day ceremony as a gesture of good will.

655
00:40:00,041 --> 00:40:05,160
The White House credits the Truman administration with starting the ritual, although some historians

656
00:40:05,161 --> 00:40:11,760
believe it dates back to Abraham Lincoln.

657
00:40:11,761 --> 00:40:15,040
It's up to specially selected breeders to raise the lucky turkey.

658
00:40:15,041 --> 00:40:19,240
In 2003, the task fell to Jim Stockham and Tom Fix.

659
00:40:19,880 --> 00:40:23,960
We wanted to see birds that had a bit of a personality.

660
00:40:24,120 --> 00:40:26,680
We wanted to see birds that would strut, that would gobble.

661
00:40:27,460 --> 00:40:32,040
The men settled on two birds, one for the ceremony and one as an alternate.

662
00:40:32,041 --> 00:40:34,520
They were named Stars and Stripes.

663
00:40:35,660 --> 00:40:40,660
Just before Thanksgiving, the men and their birds checked into the historic Hotel Washington,

664
00:40:41,140 --> 00:40:42,900
just across the street from the White House.

665
00:40:42,901 --> 00:40:48,140
That evening, we couldn't believe that a couple of guys from southwest Missouri

666
00:40:48,141 --> 00:40:53,720
had the entire top floor of the Hotel Washington as a turkey barn.

667
00:40:54,100 --> 00:40:57,780
On the big day, schoolchildren, turkey industry dignitaries,

668
00:40:58,280 --> 00:41:00,940
and a gaggle of press assembled in the Rose Garden

669
00:41:00,941 --> 00:41:04,020
and waited for the president to arrive to pardon a turkey.

670
00:41:04,900 --> 00:41:06,040
Starrs wasn't impressed.

671
00:41:06,580 --> 00:41:08,900
He was eating bugs and doing what turkeys do,

672
00:41:08,901 --> 00:41:12,420
and I thought it was quite comical

673
00:41:12,421 --> 00:41:15,160
because there was a lot of pomp and circumstance

674
00:41:15,161 --> 00:41:17,840
surrounding something that that turkey couldn't care less about.

675
00:41:17,841 --> 00:41:19,680
President of the United States.

676
00:41:24,920 --> 00:41:25,960
Thank you all.

677
00:41:27,380 --> 00:41:29,140
And welcome to the Rose Garden.

678
00:41:30,100 --> 00:41:32,280
Our guest of honor, Stars the turkey.

679
00:41:32,281 --> 00:41:40,560
Sounds like Stars wants to give the speech.

680
00:41:40,561 --> 00:41:46,060
The entire time that the president was giving his speech in the Rose Garden, the turkey

681
00:41:46,061 --> 00:41:47,060
would gobble.

682
00:41:47,061 --> 00:41:52,140
We're honoring the beginning of a holiday season.

683
00:41:52,141 --> 00:41:56,680
And now it's time to grant a little compassion to our guest of honor.

684
00:41:56,681 --> 00:42:01,400
By virtue of the presidential pardon, Stars will live out his days there at Kidwell Farm

685
00:42:01,401 --> 00:42:04,800
in Virginia, and so he won't be alone.

686
00:42:05,860 --> 00:42:07,880
I hereby pardon stripes as well.

687
00:42:08,920 --> 00:42:12,760
Pardon in hand, stars and stripes were escorted to a local farm in Virginia

688
00:42:12,761 --> 00:42:14,740
to live out their lives in peace.

689
00:42:15,720 --> 00:42:20,040
In 2007, the pardoned birds were put aboard a United Airlines jet

690
00:42:20,041 --> 00:42:23,500
where they flew first class to the Walt Disney World Resort in Florida.

691
00:42:24,280 --> 00:42:27,980
There they served as honorary grand marshals at Disney's Main Street Parade.

692
00:42:27,981 --> 00:42:33,980
Then, it was off to live out the rest of their lives at the Disney parks.

693
00:42:33,981 --> 00:42:39,980
Back at Indiana's Strongbow Inn, turkeys fill a more traditional role.

694
00:42:39,981 --> 00:42:47,980
And as the main course is served, maybe it's time to appreciate everything the ancient bird has given us.

